hi it's michelle from lab muffin beauty  science chemistry phd and skincare nerd  and lover of vitamin c  today i'm doing a collaboration with  beck winter who is a melvin based  youtuber she does tons of product  reviews and  if you know me if you ask me for a  product recommendation  i usually don't have very many beck  tries out  all of the products and does really  in-depth reviews she's very  science-based as well  and so if you want product  recommendations she is a fantastic place  to go to so  check her out her links in the  description we also did another video on  our favorite vitamin c products which  you can find on her channel as well hi  everyone my name is beckwinter and i'm a  fellow skincare  nerd i love skincare i love testing it  out and i also like the science behind  skincare because i think it's important  to melt those two worlds together so  that you can make an informed decision  as to what products and ingredients are  right for you and your skin  so we both get a lot of questions on  vitamin c so we thought we'd get  together and  give you our answers so the first  question is can vitamin c be used in the  morning because i read you shouldn't use  it in your am routine  beck what do you say absolutely  i love vitamin c particularly ascorbic  acid because it's such a great  antioxidant  and it really will benefit an am routine  because of its antioxidant qualities  particularly when you pair it with a  really nice sunscreen  i completely agree vitamin c is  fantastic for protecting your skin  against uv  and that's actually where most of the  first studies on vitamin c and skin were  it was all about protecting your skin  against the sun and reducing the amount  of sunburn you got  so it's definitely fantastic for daytime  use the antioxidants are great for  soaking up any free radicals that tend  to form  so free radicals are substances that  have unpaired electrons  and they're really really reactive so if  they're in your skin they tend to react  with things like dna  things in your cells and that's part of  what causes your skin to age  so having that in your skin helps  protect against photo aging it protects  against regular aging as well so any  free radicals that  naturally form from things that your  body does great for morning and night  why does  l ascorbic acid irritate my skin so the  quick answer to that is  it's an acid acids are irritating um  acids are low ph  that means that they have lots of  hydrogen ions and these can just  um it's like an acid burn it's a  chemical burn it reacts with your skin  ascorbic acid also has to be at a low ph  otherwise it doesn't get into your skin  it's a very stubborn molecule to get  through your skin and into the living  layers where it can have a biological  effect so if it irritates your skin  maybe you could look at using lower  percentage product  or a higher ph product or just a gentler  formula and over on my channel where we  are sharing our favorite vitamin c  serums i do have one that is a low  percentage l-ascorbic acid serum i  believe it's five percent and it seems  to be pretty well tolerated  by those of you with really sensitive  skin it's also paired with cintella as  the attica so that might help to  buffer against any irritation that you  may experience but it seems to be really  well tolerated so definitely check that  one out if you do want to use l-ascorbic  acid does  price matter i see some vitamin c serums  that are so affordable and others that  are over the top expensive  does expensive equal better so beck  you've tried everything in every price  range what do you think  um honestly for me most of my favorites  are very affordable or they're kind of  mid-priced  i don't have too many that are really  expensive that i'm willing to pay a lot  of money for  in saying that i would like to try the  ce folic acid from  skinceuticals but that is so expensive  and i just  i cannot bring myself to commit to it  because i think to myself  if it's going to do the exact same thing  in terms of vitamin c it's going to help  to stimulate collagen production  provide antioxidants for the skin and  also help to fade hyperpigmentation  why would i spend more money on it when  i get the exact same results from more  affordable products  no that's a great answer  i have i have nothing to add can you mix  vitamin c with sunscreen  um just don't mix things with sunscreen  for sunscreen it's really important that  it forms  an even film on your skin um you don't  want to mix it with anything because  firstly you dilute the protection and  secondly you might end up not mixing it  thoroughly enough and so you end up with  some spots that have enough sunscreen  some spots that have no sunscreen  so yeah don't mix it with things  layering rather than mixing  with sunscreens when it comes to  sunscreen think of it as medicinal  and just don't don't mess with it my  vitamin c turns my hands yellow um so  the reason for that is vitamin c  l ascorbic acid it slowly degrades into  erythrulose  which is a sugar which works much like  dha and fake tan so it's just fake tan  it's not like it's staining  your skin forever it's not like it's  giving you more pigmentation  it is just a fake tan thing which reacts  with the top layers of your skin  and it makes it go a little bit darker  so to avoid this i mean if it's turning  your hands yellow then it's probably not  a massive issue so wash your hands  immediately afterwards with soap and  water  um if it's turning your skin yellow  though like your face  then it's probably a good idea to layer  an oil-based  product on top and try to limit that  oxidation  you won't be able to completely undo it  but also if you apply your vitamin c  all over your face then hopefully that  gives you more of a rosy glow rather  than patches of darker  um fake tan i like that tip  if you rub it everywhere then it looks  like you intentionally meant to do that  to your skin  make it even make it even yeah if your  vitamin c is acting as like free fake  tan  go with it which is better a dedicated  vitamin c serum and niacinamide serum or  one product that uses both  i think it really depends on the formula  because if it is a really well made  formula  you have yourself a really nice hybrid a  multi-faceted product where you're going  to benefit equally well from the  niacinamide and the vitamin c  but if it's a really poorly made formula  then you're not going to benefit really  from either one of the actives can you  use vitamin c with retinol  yes can you use vitamin c with acid yes  but  um you do risk the you do risk burning  your face off a bit  because they're all very irritating so  that is pretty much the only caveat i  have have you ever used  like vitamin c with retinol or acids  before i have but i tend to use vitamin  c's during the day  um as opposed to the night if i do want  to use the vitamin c in the night i tend  to make sure that  the ingredient pairings that i have are  not going to be very irritating for the  skin  so for example i wouldn't use a 30  l-ascorbic acid serum with a really  strong retinoid or acid  yeah maybe if you do want to use a  vitamin c with these  sorts of ingredients maybe look for one  of the derivatives  and then save your vitamin c your profit  l ascorbic acid for another night  thoughts on natural vitamin c serum well  firstly l ascorbic acid is natural  or nature identical i think having the  known percentage of the vitamin c  is really important and i mean with  vitamin c you generally want something  5 10 15 and with the extracts the  natural extracts which have vitamin c  they tend not to be included in very  high concentrations so i don't think you  get that  that quantity of vitamin c on the other  hand there is also the fact that with  natural plant extracts they do have a  mix of antioxidants in them  and that tends to be really good for  keeping other antioxidants stable so  vitamin c is notoriously unstable so  chucking in other antioxidants in there  like vitamin e and ferulic acid keeps a  stable so having the plant extract  sometimes might keep it a bit more  stable  which might be a consideration but i  would probably look for a natural  in like a natural serum that has both  the l-ascorpic acid at a known  concentration plus plant extracts in  that case rather than just relying on  the plant extract for the vitamin c  so my problem when it comes to natural  vitamin c is is the fact that  in terms of if you did a qualitative  analysis  and you looked at the amount of vitamin  c in  one batch of carrot seed oil for example  and another batch of carrot seed oil  there is really such great variation  between  the amount of vitamin c in both products  so  what results you get from one product  you may not be guaranteed to get in  another product  and again because the concentrations are  so low  will it actually help in terms of  hyperpigmentation  probably not yeah i agree i like knowing  the percentage  um of something like vitamin c is  vitamin c supposed to sting or itch the  ordinary  one in silicone feels gritty and itchy  so this formula is one of the anhydrous  formulas this is where you have  l ascorbic acid crystals in an oil base  and that oil base protects it from water  from oxygen from light and that stops it  from oxidizing as quickly and breaking  down and becoming useless  so with these ones the problem is  because it's not dissolved  that's why it feels gritty it's like  little tiny crystals depending on the  brand  they might be different sizes of tiny so  generally you want  as small crystals as possible because  it'll dissolve faster on your skin  and so you end up with more even vitamin  c you won't get hot spots the problem  with the itching  is probably the creation of those hot  spots this sort of formula  is probably one of the most irritating  types out there  if your skin is too irritated with these  silicone-based formulas i'd recommend  just looking for a completely different  type of product so maybe a water-based  serum  maybe one of the derivatives and avoid  this sort of  solid l-ascorbic acid formula i feel as  though the vitamin c products that are  housed in silicones  tend to have really high amounts of  vitamin c so  that's a lot of vitamin c for your skin  that can definitely add to the  irritation that you can experience as  well yeah it's a really good point about  the high percentages i don't think i've  seen one with a low percentage and  yeah if you have high percentage acid  it's going to be irritating yeah  is vitamin c really needed in a skincare  routine or can you get a serum that  includes it in lower doses  i feel as though we're going to have two  different answers right now because for  me  vitamin c is an absolute staple but i do  deal with hyperpigmentation that  constantly needs to be treated  and post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation  in particular is quite stubborn and  really hard to treat  so i'm always on top of that vitamin c  my answer would be  um it depends on what your skin needs  are so if you have  massive hyperpigmentation problems where  that's one of your very very top  concerns  like for back then yeah vitamin c is  fantastic  you probably want to include it every  day to get rid of existing  hyperpigmentation and get rid of  anything that's forming  um if it's not such a big problem if  you're mostly looking for the  antioxidant protection  if you're looking for um for creating  more collagen  then there's a lot of other ingredients  that can do that too and so you might  want to incorporate it at a lower  percentage so yeah um everyone's  different it really depends on what your  skin needs are and that is it  for questions so i hope you enjoyed this  video if you did you can click the like  click the subscribe um you can also  check out all of our other videos you  can check out my videos through my  channel you can check out beck's videos  through her channel which i've linked in  the description  our instagram handles are up here so  follow us if you want to find out more  about science-based skin care if you  have any extra questions  on vitamin c drop them in the comments  and we'll try to get to them  and yeah see you next time  you

